      "burnthrough": "Burnthrough",
      "butt": "Butt",
      "butt_joint": "Butt Joint",
      "butt_lap_corner_edge_t_joint_position_": "Butt, lap, corner, edge, T-joint. Position codes 1F/2F/3F/4F (flat, horizontal, vertical, overhead). Quiz mode to match symbol → joint.",
      "buy_a_welder_watch_hours_of_youtube_we": "Buy a welder, watch hours of YouTube (Weld.com, Tim Welds, Pacific Arc Tig), practice on scrap. When ready, walk into an AWS-accredited test facility and pay to test. Pass D1.1 = certified, regardless of how you learned.",
      "by_lunch_you_have_not_welded_anything_": "By lunch you have not welded anything. You have learned where the eyewash station is, where the fire extinguishers live, what the alarm signals mean, where to clip your harness when you go up. You have watched Maria run a 6G stainless joint that you could not have done if your life depended on it. She makes it look like she's painting a wall.",
      "calipers_depth_gauge_for_measuring_def": "Calipers + depth gauge for measuring defect dimensions.",
      "cameras_projected_light_pattern_build_": "Cameras + projected light pattern build a 3D model of the part in real-time. Robot uses this to plan path. Most advanced + expensive systems ($50-200K added cost).",
      "cap_hand": "Cap hand: ",
      "capital_cost_30k_150k_per_system_setup": "Capital cost ($30K-150K per system). Setup time per joint can negate speed advantage on small batches. Limited to specific pipe sizes + joint configurations. Doesn't handle joint misalignment well (manual welder can compensate).",
      "carbon_equivalent": "🧮 Carbon Equivalent",
      "carbon_equivalent_ce_predicts_crack_ri": "🧮 Carbon Equivalent (CE) — predicts crack risk",
      "carbon_stainless_aluminum_exotics_haz_": "Carbon, stainless, aluminum, exotics + HAZ + PWHT",
      "carcinogenic_from_stainless_welding_fu": "Carcinogenic. From stainless welding fume (hot Cr → Cr-VI). OSHA-regulated since 2006.",
      "career_impact_framing": "💡 Career-impact framing",
      "career_path": "💰 Career path",
      "career_pathway_sections": "Career pathway sections",
      "career_pathways": "Career Pathways",
      "career_pathways_2": "Career Pathways",
      "career_preserving_habits": "💡 Career-preserving habits",
      "career_stories_mode": "Career Stories mode",
      "careercenter_maine_mainecareercenter_g": "CareerCenter Maine (mainecareercenter.gov) lists open welding positions statewide. Indeed and ZipRecruiter aggregate shop postings. Most large employers (BIW, Cianbro, Pratt) have dedicated career portals where you can apply directly without a recruiter. Many small-town shops still rely on word-of-mouth — walking in and asking is not unusual or unwelcome in this trade.",
      "careers_in_robotic_automated_welding": "💼 Careers in robotic + automated welding",
      "careers_in_robotic_welding": "💼 Careers in robotic welding",
      "cassidy_m": "Cassidy M.",
      "ce_c_mn_6_cr_mo_v_5_ni_cu_15": "CE = C + Mn/6 + (Cr + Mo + V)/5 + (Ni + Cu)/15",
      "ce_calculator": "🧮 Work it out — enter a composition",
      "ce_caveat": "CE is one input, not the whole decision. Section thickness, joint restraint and the hydrogen level of your consumables all move the preheat requirement. Check the governing code (AWS D1.1 Annex) before setting a procedure.",
      "ce_formula_for_crack_susceptibility": "CE formula for crack-susceptibility",
      "ce_is_a_weighted_sum_of_alloying_eleme": "CE is a weighted sum of alloying elements that estimates how susceptible a steel is to hydrogen-induced cracking + martensite formation. Different formulas for different steel families. The most common in welding is the IIW (International Institute of Welding) formula:",
      "centerline_of_bead_longitudinal_center": "Centerline of bead (longitudinal centerline cracks) or crater (at end of run when arc breaks).",
      "challenge_complete": "✓ Challenge complete",
      "cheap_simple_no_specialized_equipment": "Cheap, simple, no specialized equipment.",
      "chemistry_different_additives_mn_si_ti": "= Chemistry — different additives (Mn, Si, Ti, Al, Zr) for different needs",
      "choose_the_meaning": "Choose the meaning",
      "choose_the_qualification": "Choose the qualification: ",
      "chromium_carbide_precipitation": "Chromium-carbide precipitation",
      "cianbro_pittsfield_statewide": "Cianbro (Pittsfield + statewide)",
      "class_hulls_and_watched": "-class hulls and watched ",
      "clean_surface_apply_colored_or_fluores": "Clean surface → apply colored or fluorescent penetrant → let it seep into defects → wipe excess → apply developer (white powder) → defects show as colored or fluorescent lines.",
      "click_each_item_as_you_put_it_on": "Click each item as you put it on",
      "coated_electrodes_e6010_6011_6013_7018": "Coated electrodes (E6010, 6011, 6013, 7018, 7024). No shielding gas (flux coating provides). Available in 1/16\" to 3/16\" diameters.",
      "coating_type_recommended_current_see_t": "= Coating type + recommended current — see table below",
      "code_book_acceptance_criteria": "Code book + acceptance criteria: ",
      "code_quality_welds_class_a_works_at_an": "Code-quality welds (Class A). Works at any practical depth. Can use any process (TIG, MIG, Stick). Highest pay.",
      "codes_standards": "Codes + Standards",
      "codes_standards_2": "Codes + Standards",
      "cold_cracking_hydrogen_induced_crackin": "❄ Cold Cracking (Hydrogen-Induced Cracking, HIC)",
      "cold_lap": "Cold Lap",
      "cold_water_long_shifts_4_8h_underwater": "Cold water + long shifts (4-8h underwater is common). Dry suits, hot-water suits, and constant monitoring required.",
      "cold_weld_insufficient_fusion": "🧊 Cold weld (insufficient fusion)",
      "cold_weld_no_bond": "cold weld, no bond",
      "collaborative_cobots": "🤝 Collaborative cobots",
      "collaborative_cobots_universal_robots_": "🤝 Collaborative cobots (Universal Robots, etc.)",
      "collaborative_robots_cobots_are_lighte": "Collaborative robots (\"cobots\") are lighter, slower, force-limited robots designed to work alongside humans without safety cages. Easier to program (often by guiding the arm with your hand to teach positions). Used widely for low-volume welding in small shops that couldn't afford traditional robot cells.",
      "coming_soon": "Coming Soon",
      "commercial_dive_school": "commercial dive school",
      "commercial_dive_welding_work_in_maine_": "Commercial dive-welding work in Maine is rare — most opportunities are Gulf Coast offshore oil, Pacific Northwest fishing/marine, or pipeline work nationwide. A Maine kid pursuing this path will likely relocate. Bath Iron Works does occasional underwater hull inspection but most repair is dry-docked. Best entry: get topside experience at BIW or Cianbro, then apply to dive school out of state.",
      "commercial_diver_welder": "commercial diver-welder",
      "commercial_underwater_welders_earn_80_": "Commercial underwater welders earn $80-200K+ but they earn every penny. The work is dangerous, the certifications stack up, and the physics are unforgiving — water cools your weld 5-8 times faster than air, dissolved hydrogen embrittles the steel, and the arc itself is electrically conductive in saltwater. Two techniques dominate: WET (you and the torch are both in the water) and HYPERBARIC (a sealed dry chamber keeps water out).",
